\section{Agents}\label{sec:agents}
All non-random agents avoid traps and know what the actions do. What they have to find out are the bindings, and in one experiment the rule type. Table~\ref{tab:info} lists what each agent is given, which is the main thing to keep in mind when reading the results.

\textbf{Reactive.} Breadth-first-search heuristics on the current observation plus a single lose-shift bit. It keeps no record of what it tried.

\textbf{Hypothesis-driven.} The agent holds a confidence for each candidate binding (key color, door color), (switch color, chasm color) and for ``blocks are pushable''. A correct prediction adds $\alpha=0.6$ and marks the hypothesis confirmed; a wrong one subtracts $\beta=0.6$ and rejects it after two contradictions. Probabilities are normalized over the non-rejected candidates for a target, and a key already confirmed for one door is excluded from the others (the \emph{exclusivity} assumption). The state is recomputed from episodic memory at every decision, so memory capacity controls what is remembered. Decisions are made over macro-actions (go to the target, pick up or swap a key, test the held key on a door, toggle a switch, push a block) scored by
\begin{equation}
U(a)=R(a)+\lambda\,\mathrm{IG}(a)-\mu\,c(a),\label{eq:u}
\end{equation}
with expected reward $R=V\,p$, information term $\mathrm{IG}=\sum H_b(p)$ and cost $c$ equal to path length
where $p$ is the probability that the tested hypothesis is true, $H_b$ is the binary entropy, $V=3$ per gate and $10$ for the target, $\lambda=0.5$ and $\mu=0.05$. When a test settles the hypothesis directly, $H_b(p)$ is the expected information gain of that test. The constants were set by hand, not tuned per condition.

\textbf{Hypothesis-adaptive (new).} The hard exclusivity rule is replaced by a prior. A key (or switch) that is already confirmed for one target keeps weight $\rho=0.05$ for other targets instead of weight $0$, so it is tried last but not never. As soon as one key is confirmed for two different targets the prior is discarded for the rest of the episode. Exclusivity is therefore the default belief but can be overturned by evidence. The method adapts to \emph{detectable} violations only; it does not address misspecification that never shows up in the feedback.

\textbf{PSRL.}~\cite{strens2000,osband2013} Same evidence and same macro-actions, but no confidences and no information bonus: the agent samples one complete binding consistent with every outcome so far, keeps it while it stays consistent, resamples when it offers no progress, and acts as if it were true. In the vault, the hypothesis agent is the explicit set of all codes consistent with the feedback; it presses the lever most likely at the current position.

\textbf{Learned agents.} All learned agents use PPO~\cite{schulman2017} implemented in JAX~\cite{jax2018}, feedforward (FF) or with a GRU~\cite{cho2014}. \emph{Macro} agents choose among exactly the macro-actions above; their features are the macro type, the colors involved, the path cost, the last macro and its outcome, and nothing about beliefs. \emph{Macro-GRU+belief} is the matched-information control: the same agent with two more features per candidate, the expected value $R$ and the information gain IG that the hypothesis tracker computes, which also carries the templates and the exclusivity rule. \emph{Generic} agents choose among generic skills only (go to a key, switch, door, block or target; INTERACT; PUSH), so the test-the-key structure has to be learned. \emph{Primitive} PPO acts on a CNN encoding of the grid with potential-based shaping~\cite{ng1999}. Macro agents are trained for 12k episodes on the mixed-difficulty distribution and 5k on each regime's training distribution, and a separate model is trained per regime and per seed. Vault agents are trained for 30k episodes on the small codes only.

