\section{Protocol and Statistics}\label{sec:prop}
\textbf{Belief view and decomposition.} Let $\theta$ be the hidden rule table, $h_t$ the history and $b_t=P(\theta\mid h_t)$ the belief. The task is a partially observable decision problem whose only unobserved part is $\theta$~\cite{kaelbling1998}, so an optimal policy exists that depends on the history only through $(o_t,b_t)$. A learned policy without $b_t$ must carry an equivalent summary in its recurrent state. Our tracker supplies an approximate $\hat b_t$, so this motivates the design but guarantees nothing. With $S_H$, $S_L$, $S_{L+b}$ the success of the tracker agent, a learned agent, and the learned agent given $\hat b_t$, the advantage decomposes exactly: $S_H-S_L=(S_{L+b}-S_L)+(S_H-S_{L+b})$, an \emph{information gap} plus a \emph{learning gap}; the matched control estimates the first term.


Every cell of a table uses 30 evaluation seeds with 30 freshly generated environments each. For agents that have no trained weights the unit of analysis is the evaluation seed. For a learned agent it is the \emph{trained model}: evaluation seed $s$ uses model $s \bmod m$, so each of the $m$ models is run on $30/m$ evaluation seeds, and the model's score is its mean over those. The reason is that a hundred environments evaluated with one fixed network measure that network, not the training procedure; treating them as independent runs would make the intervals far too narrow. Intervals are 95\% percentile bootstraps over units, applied separately inside each agent when two agents are compared~\cite{efron1993}. Tests are Welch's $t$~\cite{welch1947} on unit scores, effect sizes are Cohen's $d$~\cite{cohen1988} with the pooled standard deviation, and the $p$-values of the @{NPRIMARY} pre-declared comparisons of Tables~\ref{tab:p1}--\ref{tab:p4} are adjusted together with Holm's method~\cite{holm1979}. We report the absolute difference of success rates, its interval, the adjusted $p$-value and $d$ for each of them; in the text, only the difference and interval are quoted, with the adjusted $p$ added when it is not below 0.05. Per-model results for every learned agent are in \texttt{paper/tables/learned\_spread.csv} and appear as dots in Figs.~\ref{fig:regimes} and~\ref{fig:vault}. Primitive-action PPO is run with fewer seeds and environments because its inference is slow. Where an agent succeeds in every episode we also give the 95\% upper bound $3/N$ on its failure rate after $N$ episodes (the ``rule of three''): a perfect score in a finite synthetic sample is not evidence of general reliability.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:one}
Let one door have $n$ equally likely candidate keys, exactly one of which opens it. (i)~An agent that never retests a failed key makes $\mathbb{E}[F_{\mathrm{elim}}]=(n-1)/2$ failed tests. (ii)~A stateless lose-shift agent that starts with a uniformly random key and, after each failure, tries a key drawn uniformly from the $n-1$ keys other than the one in hand makes $\mathbb{E}[F_{\mathrm{ls}}]=(n-1)^2/n$ failed tests. The excess $\mathbb{E}[F_{\mathrm{ls}}]-\mathbb{E}[F_{\mathrm{elim}}]=(n-1)(n-2)/(2n)$ is zero for $n=2$ and grows linearly in $n$. (iii)~In the combination vault, an agent that eliminates inconsistent codes makes $\sum_{p=0}^{k-1}(L-p-1)/2$ expected resets.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
(i)~The order in which keys are tried is a uniform random permutation, so the position of the correct key, which equals the number of failures before success, is uniform on $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ with mean $(n-1)/2$. (ii)~The first test fails with probability $(n-1)/n$. After a failure the held key is known to be wrong, so the next draw is uniform over $n-1$ keys of which exactly one is correct; the number of further attempts until success is geometric with success probability $1/(n-1)$, and so it contains $n-2$ failures on average. Hence $\mathbb{E}[F_{\mathrm{ls}}]=\frac{n-1}{n}\,(1+(n-2))=\frac{(n-1)^2}{n}$, and the excess is $\frac{(n-1)^2}{n}-\frac{n-1}{2}=\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2n}$. (iii)~At position $p$ the code is injective, so $L-p$ levers remain possible and they are equally likely a priori; (i) applies with $n=L-p$, and the presses that merely replay the already-known prefix are not failures. Summing over $p=0,\dots,k-1$ gives the stated total.
\end{proof}
For the grid we use $n$ = the number of keys reachable before the door opens, because that, not the nominal color count, is the set the agent can actually choose from.

